    <h2>One Region, Four Towns That Don't Match</h2>

    <p>The Shoals holds just under 149,000 people across two counties, and the Tennessee River runs straight through the middle of it. That river is not scenery — it's the line that sorts the whole market. North of the river is <a href="/locations/florence/">Florence</a>, the largest town at about 41,700, the seat of Lauderdale County, home to the University of North Alabama and blocks of historic housing that predate the Second World War. South of the river, in Colbert County, sit three more towns that share a county and almost nothing else. A contractor who works the region is really working four sub-markets stapled together by a couple of bridges.</p>

    <p>Here is how the towns actually differ, because the difference is the entire reason per-town pages beat one regional page:</p>

    <ul class="trades-list-block">
      <li><strong>Florence</strong> — Lauderdale County, north bank, the biggest and oldest-anchored town. UNA, a research-heavy customer base, and historic districts around Wood Avenue and Walnut Street full of the kind of housing that generates real trade work, not just routine service.</li>
      <li><strong>Muscle Shoals</strong> — Colbert County, south bank, about 17,900 and growing. Newer subdivisions north of US-43, a median household income near $75,894, and a faster, more transactional customer who searches and calls rather than researching for a week.</li>
      <li><strong>Sheffield</strong> — Colbert County, south bank, the smallest of the four at roughly 9,340 and the oldest housing stock, with a median household income closer to $39,733. This is repair-and-replace country — mid-century systems, historic-district cottages, and a customer who cares more that you'll show up than what your logo looks like.</li>
      <li><strong>Tuscumbia</strong> — Colbert County, the county seat, home to Ivy Green and the Helen Keller story. It's the fourth of the towns locals count as the Shoals and part of any real service footprint here, even though it's the quietest of the four for contractor search.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Those aren't cosmetic differences. The income gap between Muscle Shoals and Sheffield is roughly $36,000 a year across a bridge you can walk. A plumbing problem in a Muscle Shoals house built in 2011 and a plumbing problem in a Sheffield house built in 1958 are different jobs with different price conversations. Your website should say so — town by town, trade by trade.</p>

